1
00:00:00,322 --> 00:00:03,687
Rush hour: forty people in this carriage
are streaming music over the same

2
00:00:03,697 --> 00:00:05,397
slice of radio.

3
00:00:05,621 --> 00:00:08,221
So why don't you ever
hear your neighbour's?

4
00:00:08,731 --> 00:00:11,571
You might think pairing gives
your earbuds a private channel,

5
00:00:11,581 --> 00:00:13,556
like an invisible wire.

6
00:00:13,660 --> 00:00:14,994
There's no such thing.

7
00:00:15,004 --> 00:00:17,980
Yours share every channel
with everyone in here.

8
00:00:18,461 --> 00:00:22,251
It's one band, at two point four
gigahertz, shared with café Wi-Fi

9
00:00:22,261 --> 00:00:24,511
and even microwave ovens.

10
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:28,385
Classic Bluetooth slices it
into seventy-nine channels.

11
00:00:28,848 --> 00:00:30,538
And it never stays on one.

12
00:00:30,548 --> 00:00:33,998
It jumps channel up to sixteen
hundred times a second.

13
00:00:34,261 --> 00:00:37,826
The jumps look random, but they follow
a timetable your phone works out

14
00:00:37,836 --> 00:00:39,961
from its address and its clock.

15
00:00:40,144 --> 00:00:43,059
Your earbuds work out the same
one, so they're always listening

16
00:00:43,069 --> 00:00:45,444
in the right place at the right moment.

17
00:00:45,998 --> 00:00:49,798
Still, someone else's packet will
sometimes land on your channel.

18
00:00:49,948 --> 00:00:53,138
So every packet starts with
a name tag: a code made from

19
00:00:53,148 --> 00:00:54,798
your phone's address.

20
00:00:54,928 --> 00:00:57,368
Your earbuds only open up for that code.

21
00:00:57,378 --> 00:01:00,078
Your neighbour's music
carries a different one.

22
00:01:00,581 --> 00:01:04,531
And sometimes two packets hit the
same channel at the same instant.

23
00:01:04,609 --> 00:01:08,084
Bluetooth's own rulebook calls
that a strong likelihood.

24
00:01:08,298 --> 00:01:09,688
They garble each other.

25
00:01:09,698 --> 00:01:13,188
Your earbuds don't confirm, so your
phone sends it again on the next hop,

26
00:01:13,198 --> 00:01:14,998
milliseconds later.

27
00:01:15,141 --> 00:01:18,581
And a fraction of a second of music
waits in reserve, so you don't hear

28
00:01:18,591 --> 00:01:19,766
the gap.

29
00:01:20,283 --> 00:01:23,473
Cafés add a bigger
problem: Wi-Fi doesn't hop.

30
00:01:23,483 --> 00:01:26,608
A network camps on about
twenty of those channels.

31
00:01:26,818 --> 00:01:28,533
So your phone keeps score.

32
00:01:28,543 --> 00:01:31,008
Channels that keep failing are
dropped from the timetable,

33
00:01:31,018 --> 00:01:33,318
though it always keeps at least twenty.

34
00:01:33,905 --> 00:01:36,830
So your earbuds never had a private line.

35
00:01:36,884 --> 00:01:40,574
They have their own timetable,
a name tag and a second chance,

36
00:01:40,584 --> 00:01:43,184
up to sixteen hundred times a second.

37
00:01:43,482 --> 00:01:45,622
And if you do want your neighbour's song?

38
00:01:45,632 --> 00:01:49,357
Newer Bluetooth can share one
stream with any number of earbuds.
